  urldate = {2026-08-27},
  abstract = {Abstract             Clinical delusions, conspiracy theories, and fundamentalist or extremist views are crucially distinct phenomena; but in this chapter, the focus is on what they have in common. The chapter argues that, when they are reported by a speaker or acted upon by an agent, they encounter the interpreter’s epistemic disapproval. This is due to their being unshakable identity beliefs that are not widely endorsed and are found costly. They are identity beliefs because they contribute to the person’s identity, that is, how they see themselves in relation to their physical and social environment. They are unshakable because, even if they are sensitive to external challenges and can be defended using arguments, they are unlikely to be given up following an exchange of reasons.},
